---
import ProductionLayout from '../../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
import {MATERIALS,MODEL} from '../../simulation/engine';
---
<ProductionLayout title="Inside Tether Lab — Skyhook Industries" description="Equations, assumptions, material provenance and validation for the first playable Skyhook Tether Lab." theme="paper" noindex={true}>
  <section class="page-hero paper-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Tether Lab / Method</p><h1>Know what you’re flying.</h1><p>A playable experiment should be inspectable. Here is what the first lab calculates, what it assumes, and where its answers stop.</p><a class="cta light" href="/lab/">Open Tether Lab →</a></section>
  <section class="paper-section" id="scope"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">01 / Scope</p><div><h2>One world. Explicit limits.</h2><p class="lede">{MODEL} propagates a planar, rotating, rigid extended body in spherical Earth gravity. The globe is a view of those states, not an independent animation.</p><p class="body-copy">The first scenario is a symmetric in-space relay with two ideal, velocity-matched rendezvous. The incoming state is supplied by the scenario; no launch vehicle, acquisition system or capture shock is simulated. After a transfer, bounded chemical thrust can restore the facility’s operating radius, orbital speed and spin.</p><p class="body-copy">There is no atmospheric flight, elasticity, cable-wave propagation, creep, fatigue, radiation degradation, debris, electrodynamic propulsion, inbound-traffic recovery, lunar encounter or solar-system propagation in this build. Axial stress is a screening result for the rigid trajectory, not a certification that a flexible cable will follow it. Both hypothetical and published-fiber cases remain conditional.</p><p class="body-copy">A part of the tether crossing 120 km altitude stops the run as outside the modeled environment. A requested compressive load stops it as outside a taut-cable model. These are not simulated explosions or survivable re-entries.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="dark-section"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">02 / Dynamics</p><div><h2>The payload gets the actual velocity.</h2><p class="body-copy">The tether is integrated as 48 distributed mass cells, a 30 t hub and two 2 t terminal modules. Material density and the fixed area profile determine mass and inertia. Fuel is stored at the hub. Uniform and parabolic tapers are supported; a tapered end has 30% of the center area. An edit changes a design candidate, not hardware already in flight.</p><p class="body-copy">Gravity is evaluated at every mass sample. Forces and moments are summed about the instantaneous center of mass. Translation and attitude are advanced together. The numerical core uses metres, kilograms, seconds and radians, independently of the scene’s rendering scale.</p><pre style="overflow:auto;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.8;padding:1rem;border:1px solid #435761">rᵢ = R + qᵢ [cos θ, sin θ]
vᵢ = V + ω qᵢ [−sin θ, cos θ]
Fᵢ = −μ mᵢ rᵢ / |rᵢ|³
M R̈ = Σ Fᵢ + thrust
I θ̈ = Σ (rᵢ − R) × Fᵢ + thrust torque</pre><p class="body-copy">Capture and release re-express the same physical points about a new center of mass. A matched capture does not apply an invented braking impulse to the entire machine. Release has no ejector impulse: the payload departs with its instantaneous position and velocity and is then propagated separately.</p><p class="body-copy">Release phase is measured relative to Earth’s local vertical, not half an inertial spin. The release and radial-pass events are bracketed in time. A bounded 2 s RK4 step is independent of playback speed; decreasing the step and increasing quadrature resolution are part of the regression tests. Limit crossings are bracketed as well.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="paper-section"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">03 / Recovery</p><div><h2>No reset button for the orbit.</h2><p class="lede">The chemical controller has finite thrust, finite propellant and a declared specific impulse. Translational force and a tip-thruster couple act on the evolving state.</p><p class="body-copy">A feedback law seeks the original circular radius and angular momentum. Spin follows a circular short-rod gravity-gradient reference instead of spending propellant to suppress every natural spin oscillation. Actual dynamics still use distributed gravity. This educational controller is deliberately simple and is not fuel-optimal; the fuel bill is for this controller, not a minimum reboost requirement.</p><p class="body-copy">Propellant flow is (net translation thrust + |torque| / half-span) / (Isp × g₀). The combined thruster-force budget is bounded. When the tank empties, thrust stops. The first experiment assumes ideal steerable thrusters and omits feed-system mass, power, plume interactions and transient structural loads.</p><p class="body-copy">Readiness requires radius within 15 km, radial speed within 8 m/s, tangential speed within 12 m/s and spin within 0.5% of the reference for 60 s. It is checked again at the next lower radial pass before the second supplied rendezvous. Both delivered payloads must gain energy and have osculating perigee above 120 km. Completion applies only to this scenario and model; it is not a universal feasibility score.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="paper-section"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">04 / Materials</p><div><h2>A fiber number is not a finished tether.</h2><p class="lede">The two published profiles use manufacturer fiber/yarn data. The default factor of two is an educational assumption, not a full flight allowable.</p><div class="source-list">{MATERIALS.map(m=><article class="source"><span class="source-type">{m.source?'Published data':'Hypothetical'}</span><div><h3>{m.name}</h3><p>{m.basis}. {m.id!=='custom'&&`${m.density} kg/m³; ${(m.ultimate/1e9).toFixed(1)} GPa ultimate input.`}</p><p>{m.locator}</p>{m.source&&<a href={m.source}>Open source document ↗</a>}</div></article>)}</div><p class="body-copy">Kevlar 49 uses the 3.0 GPa conditioned-yarn figure, not the 3.6 GPa resin-impregnated-strand figure from the same table. Sources were checked on 6 September 2026. No brand endorsement is implied.</p><p class="body-copy">“Resize for radial load” separately solves the chosen area profile against an ideal lower-radial load case and adds a stated 15% area reserve. It can refuse a design whose distributed self-load cannot be supported at any area. A successful resize must still be run through the mission. Joint losses, environmental aging, redundancy and minimum manufacturing dimensions remain absent.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="dark-section"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">05 / Verification</p><div><h2>Tests, not convincing numbers.</h2><p class="body-copy">The core is tested for input bounds and version rejection, physical mass and inertia, point continuity at capture and release, energy and angular-momentum accounting across those events, conservative coast invariants, interior segment clearance, material failure, finite propellant, and time-step/quadrature convergence.</p><p class="body-copy">An independently implemented SciPy DOP853 integration supplies a 7,200 s conservative-coast reference. The JavaScript RK4 core is compared against it. The default two-delivery scenario and its no-recovery counterexample are regression tests, not evidence of a flight-qualified architecture.</p><p class="body-copy">The earlier v0.3/v0.4 project studies are retained separately. Their published mass estimates are not imported as accepted mission results. The new lab does not assume that a 4,000 km design survives a mission merely because an earlier sizing calculation produced a mass.</p><a class="cta" href="https://github.com/RelyOnFire/skyhook-industries/tree/main/tests">Inspect the tests ↗</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="paper-section"><div class="section-grid"><p class="chapter-label">06 / Display & saves</p><div><h2>The scene is a lens.</h2><p class="body-copy">Cable width, terminal markers and the atmosphere appearance are enlarged for legibility. They do not determine collisions or capture. The 3D orbital plane uses a fixed 28° display orientation; the underlying spherical-Earth dynamics are planar. Sunlight is illustrative, not an ephemeris. Earth turns at a fixed sidereal-rate approximation. The 2D view uses the same trajectory.</p><p class="body-copy">Simplified coastlines are dissolved from Natural Earth’s public-domain low-resolution data distributed with pyogrio’s test fixtures. They are schematic, not live satellite imagery, and display no country borders. <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/">Natural Earth terms ↗</a></p><p class="body-copy">Share links store bounded configuration inputs and model version in the URL fragment. JSON imports are validated and never execute code. Local saving is browser storage, not a backup service. No accounts, paid API or server-side simulation are required. The current catalogue is pinned to this model version; later material changes require a version change.</p><a class="cta light" href="/lab/">Back to the experiment →</a></div></div></section>
</ProductionLayout>
